mireille: heads-up — found a reproducible goroutine leak when combining Generator.Stream() with a background auth token refresher + ctx cancel. minimal repro below. :bug:

mireille: ```go
func repro() {
  ctx, cancel := context.WithCancel(context.Background())
  client := redwood.NewClient(tokenProvider.Current())
  // start a streaming generator (returns an iterator channel)
  gen := client.Generator("gpt-mini").Stream(ctx, Prompt("hello"))

  // cancels shortly after starting the stream
  go func() {
    time.Sleep(100 * time.Millisecond)
    cancel()
  }()

  // background auth refresher that rotates token and calls client.AuthRefresh()
  go func() {
    for {
      time.Sleep(200 * time.Millisecond)
      tokenProvider.Rotate()
      client.AuthRefresh() // triggers retry/handshake path inside stream runtime
    }
  }()

  for tok := range gen {
    _ = tok
  }
}
```

raj: can you paste the stack dump when the leak happens?

mireille: stack shows ~3 blocked goroutines stuck in the stream handshake retry loop (net/http dial + auth exchange) even after cancel. Looks like the stream's dial/handshake uses the original ctx but later code paths rely on a transport Close which we never expose.

michael: hmm — so the generator iterator doesn't provide an explicit Close() and relies on ctx cancellation, but the auth refresher races and re-enters handshake while the stream is half-closed? :eyes:

sofia: iirc we have similar fix in python SDK where Stream has .aclose()/close() that force-terminates the transport. go one just expects ctx to propagate which isn't enough when background goroutines mutate auth state.

emily: two options: (1) add an explicit Close() to the Stream iterator and document caller must call it on early exit, or (2) ensure any handshake/retry goroutines select on a per-handshake ctx that is derived from the stream instance and is canceled when the iterator is drained/canceled.

raj: (2) is safer long-term but breaks less code if we add Close() as short-term mitigation + add finalizer in client to detect leaks.

mireille: i'll prototype (2) with a failing unit test that simulates auth rotation + ctx cancel. will also include a small helper in examples demonstrating Close().

emily: sgtm. test should assert goroutine count doesn't increase after multiple iterations. Add a tiny stress loop to reproduce faster.

michael: FYI this also affects generator Resume() pattern where callers spawn background goroutines that expect stream to finish. we ought to add a small note in the generator README about ownership of contexts and background auth refresh.

mireille: created issue: https://github.com/redwood/inference/issues/1234 — will open PR with failing test and a patch. assigning to @raj for review once ready. :thumbsup:

ci-bot: build queued for mireille/stream-auth-leak-fix (pr/??)

raj: thanks — i'll review tomorrow. in the meantime can we add a small debug endpoint to list open stream handles for customer support?

sofia: support endpoint might be useful but low-privilege; maybe add an internal metric for active stream handles instead (counts per model/client).

mireille: noted. i'll add a metric in the patch and a short snippet for devex docs about Close() + ctx best practices.

emily: thanks! if you want i can help with the example in the go-sampleapps repo.

mireille: would love that. will ping when PR is up. :pray:
